wishnode.net — home page content phext (phexts/home.phext). Source of truth for the Exocortexia home page at wishnode.net. Task 34.6.6/7.7.10/7.6.10. See docs/COORDINATE-MAP.md for the full scheme.Exocortexia — Open Software for State Government | Phext, Inc.Open software for state government, built in the open.Exocortexia is a set of five sample programs for state and local offices. They help staff share public records, post meeting notes, track contracts, list public data, and take in forms. We built them for states with fewer than 5 million people, and we followed Nebraska law as we built them.What it isEach record is kept in what we call a phext. A phext is a way of storing information as plain text that both people and computers can read. Each document is its own page in the phext. You can check any page, and you can trace how it changed over time.The five programs share one core. Every change is written into a tamper-proof record — every entry is locked to the one before it, so if anyone changes a past entry, the change shows. When part of a record must be hidden, the hidden part is stored apart from the original. The original is never changed, and a reviewer can check what was hidden and why.These are sample programs. They show what we can build. They are not a live state system, and no real records are in them. All of the sample data is made up.The five applicationsRecords Desk. Find public records, hide the parts the law protects, and send the rest with a clear, fair bill. The law behind it: Nebraska public-records law (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 84-712).Minutes Publisher. Post meeting agendas and minutes on time, and prove when each one went up. The law behind it: Nebraska Open Meetings Act (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 84-1408 to 84-1414).Contract and Grant Ledger. Keep track of state contracts and check that the public list matches your own records. The law behind it: Nebraska contract law (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 84-602.04).Open Data Catalog. List your public datasets, say what each field means, and show when each list was last updated. The law behind it: A pilot idea. No single law requires it.Forms Intake Router. Take in forms, send each one to the right office, and let people track their form with a code. The law behind it: Built for public-records requests under Nebraska law (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 84-712).Verified, not assertedWe test what we claim. The suite is checked by 69 automated tests, and all 69 pass.The store held up under a test with 100,000 documents. Searches stayed fast.We tried to cheat the record log by changing a past entry. The check caught the change, both in use and after the file was saved and read back.When 500 people used one program at the same moment, answers slowed to about a second at first. We found the bottleneck and fixed it. Now the slowest answers take about 26 milliseconds — less time than a blink.The sample data is made the same way each time. Make it again, and you get the exact same files.One honest limit: we ran these speed tests on one computer in our own office, not over the public internet. They show the programs have room to spare for a small state's workload. They are not a promise about internet speed.The full test results are on this site, on the page called “Read the full test results.”SecurityA full security audit of the suite is still in progress. No audit results are posted yet. When the audit is done, its results will be posted on this site, on the page called “Security.”Ask about a pilotAsk about a pilot for your office. Use the form on this site — the link says “Ask about a pilot.” Tell us which program you are interested in and what your office needs. When you send the form, you get a tracking code. Keep it. You can use it on this site to check the state of your request at any time. We read every request, and a person from Phext, Inc. replies. We will talk through what a small pilot would look like for your office. There is no cost to ask, and asking does not sign you up for anything.Exocortexia · by Phext, Inc.Records Desk | Minutes Publisher | Contract Ledger | Data Catalog | Forms Router | Security | Ask about a pilotPhext, Inc. makes open software for public offices. This site is the home of our Exocortexia suite. To reach us, use the “Ask about a pilot” form on this site. You get a tracking code when you send it, and a person replies.Exocortexia is built by Phext, Inc. The programs are free to use and change under the MIT license. wishnode.netHow this site worksEvery word on this site is kept in a phext. A phext is a way of storing information as plain text that both people and computers can read. When we fix a word, we fix it in the phext, and the site is rebuilt from it.Each page has its own phext. The home page, each program page, the test results, and the law sheets each live in their own file of plain text. A small server program called SQ serves those files to the tools that build and run this site.Your form, your recordWhen you send a form, it goes into a phext of your own. No one else's form is in it. The site also keeps a short claim card for your form: your tracking code, the date, a proof code made from your form's words, and where your form stands.The claim card never holds your words. It points to your record and holds the proof code, so anyone can check that a form was not changed after it was sent. When the state of your form changes, a new line is added to the card. Old lines are never removed or changed.Who can do whatAnyone can read the pages. Only you can read your form, with your tracking code or by signing in. Our staff can move a form from one state to the next, and each move is written on the claim card with the role that made it. Only the site owner can change the words on the pages.Sign-in uses the same account as edit.phext.io, the editor where these programs are built. When sign-in is turned on, it shows you every form tied to your account. Until then, your tracking code does the same job.FOR TECHNICAL STAFF — not main copy. Suite: single Rust workspace, std-lib HTTP only, no framework, no async runtime. Storage: libphext phext store; hash-chained audit log; redactions as separate scrolls. Sign-in: edit.phext.io SSO via HMAC assertions; open demo mode with banner when SSO is not configured. Source repository: github.com/wbic16/Exocortexia (branch exo). Full receipts: phexts/verification.phext. Statutes: phexts/statutes.phext.Original home page text before the plain-English rewrite (provenance):
